'''Senescence''' is age-associated deterioration in biological function, or, in cellular biology, a distinct state in which a cell typically stops dividing persistently while remaining metabolically active. These meanings are related but are not identical.

== Organismal versus cellular processes ==
Organismal senescence concerns changes in survival, reproductive performance or physiological function with age. Patterns differ among species and populations, and age-associated mortality can reflect environmental as well as intrinsic factors. Cellular senescence is a stress-associated state characterized by durable proliferative arrest and context-dependent molecular and secretory changes. It differs from transient quiescence, terminal differentiation and cell death, although distinguishing states can require multiple measurements.

== Cellular mechanisms and markers ==
DNA damage, telomere dysfunction, oncogenic signaling and other stresses can induce senescence-associated programs. The p53–p21 and p16–RB pathways participate in arrest in many contexts, but no single marker is universal. Senescent cells may develop a senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP), whose composition varies by cell type, induction and time. Gorgoulis et al. (2019) provide consensus guidance emphasizing combinations of features rather than diagnosis from one biomarker. SA-β-gal activity, proliferation measures, arrest-pathway markers and secretory features should be interpreted together with controls.

== Population and experimental analysis ==
An illustrative mortality model is the Gompertz hazard <math>\mu(a)=\mu_0e^{\gamma a}</math>, with age a and fitted parameters. It is a model for some adult-age mortality patterns, not a universal biological law or a cellular mechanism. In cell experiments, distinguish a persistent arrest from a temporary response using longitudinal observation, appropriate control populations and independent markers. Tissue studies must account for heterogeneity and sampling. The 2023 hallmarks-of-aging framework includes cellular senescence among multiple interacting hallmarks; it does not imply that eliminating all senescent cells is safe or sufficient to reverse aging. Therapeutic claims require evidence from the relevant organisms and clinical contexts.

